<html xmlns:o='urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:office' xmlns:w='urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:word' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/TR/REC-html40'>
<head><title>Integration Test Document</title></head>
<body>
<table border="1" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0" style="border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;">
  <tr style="background-color: #d9d9d9;">
    <td colspan="2" align="center"><h2>INTEGRATION TEST DOCUMENT</h2></td>
    <td><b>Modules/Components Integration Points</b></td>
    <td colspan="3">Web Map UI (React) -> Analytics Engine API (/api/status) -> Open-Meteo Satellite API -> Web Data Visualization</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><b>IT-ID</b></td>
    <td align="center">WEB-IT-002</td>
    <td><b>Description</b></td>
    <td colspan="3">Verify that the Web Flood Map module successfully retrieves and integrates real-time telemetry and weather prediction data from the Analytics Engine API.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><b>Date/Time Tested</b></td>
    <td align="center">July 28, 2026<br>6:10 PM</td>
    <td><b>Type of System</b></td>
    <td colspan="3">Web Application</td>
  </tr>
  <tr style="background-color: #d9d9d9;">
    <td><b>Pre-conditions</b></td>
    <td colspan="5">System is hosted and online. Analytics Engine API and Open-Meteo API are reachable. User has navigated to the Web Admin Map Dashboard.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr style="background-color: #d9d9d9; font-weight: bold;" align="center">
    <td>Test Cycle</td>
    <td>Steps/Actions</td>
    <td>Data</td>
    <td>Expected Results</td>
    <td>Actual Results</td>
    <td>Pass/Fail</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td align="center">1</td>
    <td>1. Navigate to the Web Dashboard Map Component.<br>2. Wait for initial API fetch via React useEffect.</td>
    <td>API Endpoint: GET /api/status</td>
    <td>Web Map successfully loads. The React system initiates an HTTP request to the Analytics Engine API without timing out.</td>
    <td>Map loads, HTTP 200 OK received from API.</td>
    <td>Pass</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td align="center">2</td>
    <td>1. Click on a specific Barangay polygon (e.g., Concepcion Dos) on the Leaflet Map.</td>
    <td>Barangay Name: Concepcion Dos</td>
    <td>The React frontend successfully parses the API JSON response and maps the barangay to the correct sensor group.</td>
    <td>Barangay maps to correct sensor data and displays accurately on the web card.</td>
    <td>Pass</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td align="center">3</td>
    <td>1. View the Water Level Gauge on the selected Web Barangay card.</td>
    <td>API JSON payload (Live Water Level, Prediction)</td>
    <td>The Web UI gauge successfully translates the API math, starting at the 11m baseline, displaying the live water level and dynamic predicted timeline.</td>
    <td>Web Gauge visualizes the API payload seamlessly.</td>
    <td>Pass</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td align="center">4</td>
    <td>1. View the Weather Cards (Rain, Temp, Heat Index, Wind).</td>
    <td>API JSON payload (Weather Data)</td>
    <td>The React frontend successfully integrates and displays the external Open-Meteo weather variables parsed by the backend.</td>
    <td>Weather cards display live satellite data accurately on the web dashboard.</td>
    <td>Pass</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td align="center">5</td>
    <td>1. Hover cursor over a specific Barangay polygon (e.g., Malanday).</td>
    <td>Cursor Coordinates, Map Polygon GeoJSON</td>
    <td>The frontend successfully cross-references the GeoJSON boundary with the active API payload and highlights the polygon on the map.</td>
    <td>Polygon highlights and displays a tooltip with API-linked data instantly.</td>
    <td>Pass</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td colspan="6"><b>Comments/Remarks</b><br>Integration between the React Web Frontend and the Python Analytics Engine API is seamless. Data parsing functions exactly as mathematically intended on the web client.</td>
  </tr>
</table>
</body>
</html>
